EXT. SLED-DOG CHECKPOINT - ARCTIC DUSK

A canvas tent in a field of blue snow. A propane heater. A rack of frozen dogs' booties. Wind flaps the tent walls. Overhead, the aurora begins to spill green.

INGRID (30s, parka, frost in her eyelashes) kneels beside a team of eight huskies, rubbing a dog's paw. BAPTISTE (40s, race vet, orange snowsuit, stethoscope) checks another dog's heartbeat. A handheld radio crackles on a crate between them.

MARSHAL VOGEL (V.O.)
(radio)
Checkpoint Four, this is Race Control. Status on team nineteen.

INGRID
Race Control, team nineteen is at Checkpoint Four. Two hours rest. Then out.

MARSHAL VOGEL (V.O.)
Negative. Storm front closing. You have twenty minutes.

INGRID
(to Baptiste)
Twenty minutes?

BAPTISTE
She can't run eight dogs in twenty.

INGRID
They can.

BAPTISTE
Not that one.

He points. A big gray husky named Ash lies on his side, tongue lolling, ribs fluttering. Ingrid's face drops. She crawls to him.

INGRID
Ash. Hey. Hey, big man.

BAPTISTE
He's dehydrated. I'm pulling him from the team.

INGRID
No.

BAPTISTE
Ingrid.

INGRID
He's my lead.

BAPTISTE
He's a dog.

INGRID ^
He's my lead dog.

The radio crackles again.

MARSHAL VOGEL (V.O.)
Team nineteen, you have fifteen.

Ingrid grabs the radio.

INGRID
Race Control, scratch team nineteen.

MARSHAL VOGEL (V.O.)
Say again?

INGRID
I'm scratching. I'm staying with my dog.

A beat of static. Baptiste smiles, and brings her a cup of water for Ash. Above them the aurora breaks wide and green across the dark.
